i think if you want to talk about  recession nervousness uh you should look  uh at today's jobs report uh numbers  like this are just very much  inconsistent with uh  any kind of a recession call well you  know we have we're not denying that we  have work to do on inflation i know  times are tough  prices are too high  families are facing the cost of living  crunch but today's economic news  confirms the fact that my economic plan  is moving this country in a better  direction  let's look at the numbers the june jobs  report 372 thousand jobs added  unemployment staying steady at 3.6  percent  wages are up 5.1 percent but not even  close to keeping up with inflation with  that let's bring in our panel ben  dominic editor of thetransom.com and  host of the ben dominic podcast on fox  news radio leslie marshall democratic  strategist and jeff mason white house  correspondent for reuters since you're  on the north lawn jeff let's start with  you the state of the american economy  four months ahead of midterm election  day  well mike the labor market is strong and  that's certainly something that they  emphasize today from the white house and  and from the administration but as as  the president said in the clip that you  played that doesn't mean they don't  think there's work to do as we've  discussed before inflation is is  obviously very high and americans are  feeling that but the the advantage i  guess of the labor market being strong  is that if there is a reception a  recession it could mean that it would be  a mild one  on the other hand it could also will  likely mean that the fed will will have  a pretty hefty uh rate hike coming up so  there are pros and cons to that but  at least they see some glimmers of hope  here uh today leslie you're outside the  beltway what's the feel in terms of the  american economy  on the west coast  well not just on the west coast and not  just here on the left side of things  with my party people like seeing gas  prices come down as they have been for  days now people like seeing unemployment  low and staying steady and people like  seeing job creation and jobs report  better than it's been i'm hoping that's  going to transcend to some higher  approval ratings for the president  because all the american people see is  doom and gloom in the headlines  especially when it comes to the words  recession or inflation that still scares  them we're not out of the scary waters  yet but this is definitely good news and  i say that as a pessimist ben solid jobs  number but inflation's pretty brutal  your thoughts i think that there's very  little hope for the democratic coalition  to change the narrative  as people look at the situation around  the country  there's much more reason to be  pessimistic in this moment than there is  to be optimistic about the direction of  the economy and i don't think that  you're going to see any of that change  in time to further democratic hopes in  november  also today the president signing an  executive order on abortion he vented  his frustration with the supreme court  let's play it  the truth is today's supreme court  majority  that is playing fast and loose with the  facts i'm asking the justice department  that much like they did in the civil  rights area to do something  do everything in their power to protect  these women seeking to invoke their  rights  jeff is that just frustration from the  president since he really doesn't have  control over the supreme court yeah i  think there's a lot of frustration there  because of what you just said mike and  and broadly because he can't actually do  that much with executive orders on the  issue of abortion uh he issued the one  that we that you were just referring to  uh it does a few things it seeks to  protect access to abortions and to  contraception uh for women but the truth  is he just he doesn't have that much  authority to do that that authority is  now with the states leslie there have  been calls from the left for the  president to do something on abortion  rights is it enough  well it's not enough but he doesn't have  the power to do enough or to do more uh  certainly this is uh going to appease  some but it it's not enough it's very  scary when you heard kevin mccarthy  yesterday uh you know touching upon a  federal ban which we think republicans  will do if they get the house the senate  and the white house uh and not in the  midterms but in the general election and  of course there's a concern about  republicans uh trying to limit the  ability for women to travel interstate  even though that's currently protected  that could always be changed roe v wade  was protected and there since 1973  president's doing the best that he can  what the american people need to do if  they don't like this decision is get out  and vote in november and vote for people  that want to protect a woman's  reproductive rights ben what about that  does that energize democrats in  november's midterm elections  unfortunately virtually anybody on the  democratic side who was going to be  energized by this type of of rhetoric  was already going to be voting anyway  this is not a situation where you're  going to see some kind of explosive turn  around for them and a big part of that  has to do with the frankly extreme  position that the democratic party  occupies on this issue when you're  talking about  a nine-month spread when you're talking  about this sort of level of support for  abortion including taxpayer funding  that's something that just has not been  a major popular point for them  for some time now and i think that  joe biden and his party are running  right into the teeth of that  because it turns out that roe v wade  despite being framed as something that  was settled and popular and the like in  a lot of the media framing and the polls  over the years has not turned out to be  that popular and will not turn out and  from my perspective to be something that  changes the dynamic headed  into the midterms all right there'll be  plenty more debate about this issue  going into the fall but now it's time  for a moment in the show our viewers  look forward to winners and losers jeff  why don't you lead us off all right for  winners uh for the week i picked john  mccain the late senator and the other uh  people yesterday who received the medal  of freedom from president biden in a in  a very emotional uh and touching  ceremony and for loser of the week elon  musk and very possibly the the  shareholders of twitter after he said  tonight uh that he was pulling out of  his deal to buy uh that social media  company all right leslie your turn  i'm gonna go loser first marjorie taylor  green for once again being insensitive  to victims and victims families when she  is alluding to the highland park  shooting shooting as being a false flag  and winners well i'm going to say my  state and the governor of my state gavin  newsom is going to make insulin  affordable to those who need it all  right ben bring us home  boris johnson is my loser for this week  really a disappointment in terms of his  tenure as prime minister  though it is rather ridiculous as  mccauley told us to see the british  people go through a moralistic fit now  in the end  and then my winner for the week is  actually flo ryda and the folks at  fox and friends this morning put on an  incredible performance and included a  wonderful image of steve ducey dancing  along  to this it was just a good a good  enjoyable way to end what has been a  tough week panel thanks so much have a  beautiful weekend  hi i'm brian kilmeade i want you to do  me a favor i want you to click to  subscribe to the fox news youtube page  this is the only way that i know for  sure that you're not going to miss any  great commentary any great news bites  any great interviews coming your way on  fox you can get it all here on youtube  so subscribe right now

